# Orrery — Game state

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The build owns the shape of the game's state entirely. Choose the types, the
fields, and the modules that hold them to suit the implementation you write.

The one contract fixed over the state is the debugging and automation surface
specified for this build. Every operation that surface carries is a read or a
pose of the running game, so the state holds what those operations read,
honors what they write, and returns to its title-screen values when the
surface's `reset` is called. Beyond satisfying that surface, how the state is
organized is yours to decide.

Whatever its shape, the state carries the following, because the rest of this
specification is written against them:

- The screen being shown, of `title`, `howto`, `select`, and `editor`, the
  mode the select and editor screens serve, the highlighted item of whichever
  menu is showing, the highlighted row of the select screen, and the how-to's
  current page, which the snapshot reports as `howtoPage`.
- Per-mode progress: how many campaign challenges are unlocked, which
  challenges of each mode are solved, each challenge's records, the machine
  stashed for each challenge the session has touched, and for each mode the
  challenge most recently entered or solved, which is where its select screen
  lands.
- The challenge open in the editor, if any: its full definition in the terms
  of `specs/formats.md`, and which mode and index it came from, or that it
  was loaded directly.
- The machine being edited: every placed part with its kind, pose, length,
  track path, rise or set index, and tape, exactly the facts a solution in
  `specs/formats.md` records, plus a counter that gives each new part a
  fresh identity.
- The editor's working state: the selected part, the focus, the tape cursor,
  the live drag or lay with its targeted hex and, for a move, the hex the
  press grabbed, and the undo and redo histories of `specs/editor.md`.
- The run, when one is active: its status, the completed-cycle count, the
  fraction through the current cycle, the speed step, every mote with its
  identity, hex, type, and whether it is a fixture of a wheel, every
  filament with its weight, every arm's live pose, every grip joining a
  gripper to a mote, each set's tally, the area bank, the fault if one was
  raised, and the metrics once complete.
- The pointer position, in the stage's logical units, and whether the
  pointer is currently pressed, both refreshed from the runtime layer in
  every update.
- The simulation time, in seconds, which every update adds its `dt` to
  whatever the screen. The snapshot reports it as `simTime`.
- The runtime's mute bit, mirrored into the state every update and reported
  by the snapshot as `muted`.

Progress for both modes is carried whatever the mode being played, so the
state has one shape. Fields nothing has touched yet hold their resting
values: no challenge open, an empty editor, no run, nothing solved, and only
the first campaign challenge unlocked.
{{else}}
`src/game.ts` declares and exports the state the game is written against.
This section is that declaration: write it into `src/game.ts` exactly as it
stands here. The state is a value the engine holds and every frame replaces,
so every field and every array is declared `readonly`: a frame builds the
next state from the current one rather than writing into it, and every
reader is handed the state as `DeepReadonly<OrreryState>` in any case.

```ts
export type Screen = "title" | "howto" | "select" | "editor";

export type Mode = "campaign" | "extras";

export type MoteType =
  | "dust" | "nebula" | "comet" | "nova" | "meteor"
  | "mercury"
  | "saturn" | "jupiter" | "mars" | "venus" | "luna" | "sol"
  | "umbra" | "lumen" | "aether";

export type PartKind =
  | "arm" | "biarm" | "triarm" | "hexarm" | "piston" | "wheel" | "track"
  | "bind" | "manifold" | "triune" | "sunder"
  | "wane" | "mirror" | "ascend" | "conjoin" | "eclipse"
  | "confluence" | "dispersion" | "void"
  | "rise" | "set";

export type Instruction =
  | "grab" | "drop"
  | "rotate-cw" | "rotate-ccw"
  | "pivot-cw" | "pivot-ccw"
  | "extend" | "retract"
  | "advance" | "recede";

export type TapeCell = Instruction | null;

export type SimStatus = "running" | "paused" | "faulted" | "complete";

export type FaultKind =
  | "collision" | "torn"
  | "overextended" | "overretracted"
  | "unmounted" | "track-end" | "impossible";

export type Focus = "field" | "tape";

export interface Hex {
  readonly q: number;
  readonly r: number;
}

export interface PatternFilament {
  readonly a: Hex;
  readonly b: Hex;
  readonly weight: number;
}

export interface Molecule {
  readonly motes: readonly { readonly q: number; readonly r: number; readonly type: MoteType }[];
  readonly filaments: readonly PatternFilament[];
  readonly repeat: { readonly vector: Hex; readonly link: PatternFilament } | null;
}

export interface Challenge {
  readonly name: string;
  readonly reagents: readonly Molecule[];
  readonly products: readonly Molecule[];
  readonly permitted: readonly PartKind[];
  readonly target: number;
}

export interface PartState {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly kind: PartKind;
  readonly q: number;
  readonly r: number;
  readonly rotation: number;
  readonly length: number;
  readonly cells: readonly Hex[] | null;
  readonly closed: boolean | null;
  readonly index: number | null;
  readonly tape: readonly TapeCell[] | null;
}

export type DragState =
  | {
      readonly kind: "place";
      readonly part: PartKind;
      readonly index: number | null;
      readonly rotation: number;
      readonly length: number;
      readonly at: Hex | null;
    }
  | {
      readonly kind: "move";
      readonly part: number;
      readonly from: Hex;
      readonly at: Hex | null;
    }
  | { readonly kind: "lay"; readonly part: number; readonly end: "first" | "last" };

export interface EditorState {
  readonly parts: readonly PartState[];
  readonly nextId: number;
  readonly selected: number | null;
  readonly focus: Focus;
  readonly cursor: { readonly part: number; readonly col: number } | null;
  readonly drag: DragState | null;
  readonly undo: readonly (readonly PartState[])[];
  readonly redo: readonly (readonly PartState[])[];
}

export interface MoteState {
  readonly id: number;
  readonly q: number;
  readonly r: number;
  readonly type: MoteType;
  readonly wheel: number | null;
}

export interface SimFilament {
  readonly a: number;
  readonly b: number;
  readonly weight: number;
}

export interface Pose {
  readonly part: number;
  readonly rotation: number;
  readonly length: number;
  readonly cell: Hex;
}

export interface Grip {
  readonly part: number;
  readonly spoke: number;
  readonly mote: number;
}

export interface Metrics {
  readonly cost: number;
  readonly cycles: number;
  readonly area: number;
}

export interface SimState {
  readonly status: SimStatus;
  readonly cycle: number;
  readonly fraction: number;
  readonly speed: number;
  readonly motes: readonly MoteState[];
  readonly nextMoteId: number;
  readonly filaments: readonly SimFilament[];
  readonly poses: readonly Pose[];
  readonly grips: readonly Grip[];
  readonly tallies: readonly number[];
  readonly areaHexes: readonly Hex[];
  readonly fault: { readonly kind: FaultKind; readonly parts: readonly number[]; readonly motes: readonly number[] } | null;
  readonly metrics: Metrics | null;
}

export interface PointerState {
  readonly x: number;
  readonly y: number;
  readonly down: boolean;
}

export interface OrreryState {
  readonly screen: Screen;
  readonly mode: Mode;
  readonly menuIndex: number;
  readonly selectIndex: number;
  readonly howtoPage: number;

  readonly unlockedCount: number;
  readonly campaignSolved: readonly number[];
  readonly extrasSolved: readonly number[];
  readonly campaignRecords: readonly (Metrics | null)[];
  readonly extrasRecords: readonly (Metrics | null)[];
  readonly campaignMachines: readonly (readonly PartState[] | null)[];
  readonly extrasMachines: readonly (readonly PartState[] | null)[];
  readonly campaignLast: number;
  readonly extrasLast: number;

  readonly challenge: Challenge | null;
  readonly challengeRef: { readonly mode: Mode; readonly index: number } | null;
  readonly editor: EditorState;
  readonly sim: SimState | null;

  readonly pointer: PointerState;
  readonly simTime: number;
  readonly muted: boolean;
}
```

## The type aliases

`Screen` is the top-level state machine and `screen` names the screen the
game is showing; the solved panel and the fault display are drawn on
`editor` from `sim.status` rather than being screens. `Mode` names the
course `select` and `editor` serve. `MoteType`, `PartKind`, `Instruction`,
and `FaultKind` are the rosters of `specs/field.md`, `specs/parts.md`,
`specs/instructions.md`, and `specs/simulation.md`, and `TapeCell` adds the
blank. `Hex` is an axial coordinate. `PatternFilament`, `Molecule`, and
`Challenge` are the formats of `specs/formats.md` as data: a molecule
filament's `a` and `b` are pattern hexes, and `weight` is `1` or `3`.

## `PartState`

One placed part, carrying exactly the facts a solution records, plus `id`, a
number unique among the machine's parts for its lifetime, granted from
`editor.nextId`.

- `q`, `r` — the anchor hex. For a track this mirrors the first cell of
  `cells`.
- `rotation` — `0` to `5` as `specs/field.md` counts them, and `0` for a
  track.
- `length` — the rest length, `1` to `3`, for arms and pistons; `1` for
  everything else.
- `cells`, `closed` — the path and loop flag for a track; `null` for
  everything else.
- `index` — which reagent or product, for a rise or set; `null` for
  everything else.
- `tape` — the tape, for arms and wheels; `null` for everything else. Its
  length is trimmed: the last entry is never `null`, and an entirely blank
  tape is the empty array.

`parts` holds the machine in placement order, which is the order the tape
panel lists its rows in. Cost, the period, and what is mounted on what are
derived, never stored.

## `EditorState`

The machine and the hands on it. `selected`, `cursor.part`, and a drag's
`part` name parts by `id`. `drag` is `null` outside a press; a `place` drag
carries what the tray handed out and the ghost's live `rotation` and
`length`, a `move` drag carries the hex the press grabbed in `from` and its
target in `at`, and a `lay` drag names which end of the track it is
extending. `undo` and `redo` are the histories of
`specs/editor.md`, holding machines exactly as `parts` holds one. On
entering a challenge the editor holds the stashed machine or an empty one,
`selected` and `cursor` are `null`, `focus` is `field`, `drag` is `null`,
and both histories are empty.

## `SimState`

The whole of a run, and `null` while editing.

- `status`, `cycle`, `fraction`, `speed` — the clock of
  `specs/simulation.md`: completed cycles, the fraction through the current
  one, and the `SPEEDS` index, `DEFAULT_SPEED_INDEX` (`1`) at run start.
- `motes` — every mote, fixtures included. `q` and `r` are the mote's hex at
  the last boundary; a mote's drawn mid-cycle position is derived from its
  motion and `fraction`. `wheel` names the wheel a fixture belongs to and is
  `null` on every real mote.
- `nextMoteId` — grants identities, so a mote's `id` is stable for the run
  and never reused.
- `filaments` — every filament, by mote ids, `weight` `1` or `3`.
- `poses` — one entry per arm and wheel: its live rotation, length, and base
  cell. Parts at rest between instructions still have their entry.
- `grips` — one entry per holding gripper: the part, the spoke direction the
  gripper currently sits on, and the mote at the gripper. Constellation
  membership is derived from `filaments`.
- `tallies` — one entry per product, in product order.
- `areaHexes` — the area bank, each hex once.
- `fault`, `metrics` — set exactly when `status` is `faulted` and `complete`
  respectively, `null` otherwise.

## `OrreryState`

The whole of Orrery's state. Every field is present from the moment
`initialize` returns, and every one is plain data, so a scenario is posed by
building a state that differs in the fields posed and read back by reading
them.

- `screen`, `mode`, `menuIndex`, `selectIndex`, `howtoPage` — where the
  player is. `selectIndex` is the highlighted select row of the current mode,
  and `howtoPage` the how-to's current page, `0` whenever the screen is not
  `howto`.
- `unlockedCount` — how many campaign challenges are open, `1` at the start.
- `campaignSolved`, `extrasSolved` — solved indices, ascending, no
  duplicates.
- `campaignRecords`, `extrasRecords` — per-challenge records, `null` until a
  challenge's first completion; each metric is the session's best
  independently. The campaign arrays' length is the course length the build
  ships; the extras arrays' length is `EXTRA_COUNT` (`10`).
- `campaignMachines`, `extrasMachines` — the per-challenge stashes of
  `specs/editor.md`, `null` for a challenge never opened.
- `campaignLast`, `extrasLast` — the index each mode's select screen lands
  on, `0` until something is entered or solved.
- `challenge`, `challengeRef` — the challenge open in the editor and where
  it came from; `challengeRef` is `null` when the challenge was loaded
  directly through the surface of `specs/instrumentation.md`. Both are
  `null` away from the editor.
- `editor`, `sim` — the machine and the run.
- `pointer` — the pointer as of this frame, in stage units.
- `simTime` — accumulated simulation time, in seconds. Every `update` adds
  its `dt`, whatever the screen.
- `muted` — the game's readable copy of the engine's mute bit, which every
  `update` carries into the state it returns.

## The contract

The declaration above is what the debug surface reads and poses.

- Keep every field under its declared name, type, and meaning.
- `initialize` builds the whole state in one go, so every field is present
  by the time a frame can observe it, and none of them is optional.
- Fields you add hold derived data you can rebuild from the declared ones. A
  `reset` restores the declared fields to their title-screen values, so
  those fields are the whole of the authoritative state.
- The state handed to `update` is the whole of the game, and the state
  `update` returns is the whole of the next frame. Every value the game
  carries from one frame to the next lives in it; nothing is kept in a
  module-level variable or a closure.
- The state is handed over read-only, as `DeepReadonly<OrreryState>`, to
  `update`, to `render`, to every diagnostic source, and to every operation
  of the debug surface. `update` and the surface's poses return a new
  `OrreryState` built from the one they received; `render`, the sources, and
  `snapshot` return no state. The type is what guarantees that rendering
  changes nothing and that nothing but a transition advances the game.
{{/if}}
